w.greenpenguin.co.uk\nL29@P4: Penguin Random House is committed to a\nL30@P4: sustainable future for our business, our readers\nL31@P4: and our planet. \u0006is book is made from Forest\nL32@P4: Stewardship Council® certified paper.\nL33@P4-5: Copyrighted Materialto teachers everywhere, sung and unsung\nL34@P5: Whatever we do, we have to make our life vectors.\nL35@P5: Lines with force and direction.\nL36@P5: —LIAM NEESON AS FBI AGENT MARK FELT\nL37@P5: IN THE 2017 MOVIE OF THE SAME NAME\nL38@P5-7: Copyrighted MaterialCopyrighted Material\u001a e author acknowledges with gratitude the support\nL39@P7: of the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation in the\nL40@P7: research and writing of this book.\nL41@P7-9: Copyrighted MaterialCopyrighted MaterialCONTENTS\nL42@P9: Prologue 1\nL43@P9: CHAPTER 1\nL44@P9: Desperately Seeking Patterns 7\nL45@P9: CHAPTER 2\nL46@P9: We Are All Just Numbers\nL47@P9:  Here . . . 26\nL48@P9: CHAPTER 3\nL49@P9: The Bottom of the Bowl 64\nL50@P9: CHAPTER 4\nL51@P9: In All Probability 95\nL52@P9: CHAPTER 5\nL53@P9: Birds of a Feather 144\nL54@P9: CHAPTER 6\nL55@P9: There’s Magic in Them\nL56@P9: Matrices 176\nL57@P9: CHAPTER 7\nL58@P9: The Great Kernel Rope Trick 206\nL59@P9: CHAPTER 8\nL60@P9: With a Little Help from\nL61@P9: Physics 242\nL62@P9: CHAPTER 9\nL63@P9: The Man Who Set Back Deep\nL64@P9: Learning (Not Really) 277\nL65@P9: CHAPTER 10\nL66@P9: The Algorithm that Put\nL67@P9: Paid to a Persistent Myth 302\nL68@P9: CHAPTER 11\nL69@P9: The Eyes of a Machine 346\nL70@P9: CHAPTER 12\nL71@P9: Terra Incognita 382\nL72@P9: Epilogue 415\nL73@P9: Acknowledgments 431\nL74@P9: Notes 435\nL75@P9: Index 455\nL76@P9-11: Copyrighted MaterialCopyrighted MaterialPrologue\nL77@P11: B uried on page 25 of the July 8, 1958, issue of \u001f e New York\nL78@P11: Times was a rather extraordinary story. \u001a e headline read, “New\nL79@P11: Navy Device Learns by Doing: Psychologist Shows Embryo of Com\u0002puter Designed to Read and Grow Wiser.” \u001a e opening paragraph\nL80@P11: raised the stakes: “\u001a e Navy revealed the embryo of an electronic\nL81@P11: computer today that it expects will be able to walk, talk, see, write,\nL82@P11: reproduce itself and be conscious of its existence.”\nL83@P11: With hindsight, the hyperbole is obvious and embarrassing. But\nL84@P11: \u001f e New York Times wasn’t entirely at fault. Some of the over- the- top\nL85@P11: talk also came from Frank Rosenblatt, a Cornell University psychol\u0002ogist and project engineer. Rosenblatt, with funding from the U.S.\nL86@P11: O\u0019 ce of Naval Research, had invented the perceptron, a version of\nL87@P11: which was presented at a press conference the day before the New\nL88@P11: York Times story about it appeared in print. According to Rosenblatt,\nL89@P11: the perceptron would be the “\u0018 rst device to think as the human brain”\nL90@P11: and such machines might even be sent to other planets as “mechan\u0002ical space explorers.”\nL91@P11: None of this happened. \u001a e perceptron never lived up to the\nL92@P11: hype. Nonetheless, Rosenblatt’s work was seminal. Almost every lec\u0002turer on arti\u0018 cial intelligence (AI) today will harken back to the\nL93@P11: perceptron. And that’s justi\u0018 ed. \u001a is moment in history— the ar\u0002rival of large language models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT and its ilk\nL94@P11-12: Copyrighted MaterialWHY MACHINES LEARN\nL95@P12: 2\nL96@P12: and our response to it— which some have likened to what it must\nL97@P12: have felt like in the 1910s and ’20s, when physicists were confronted\nL98@P12: with the craziness of quantum mechanics, has its roots in research\nL99@P12: initiated by Rosenblatt. \u001a ere’s a line in the New York Times story\nL100@P12: that only hints at the revolution the perceptron set in motion: “Dr.\nL101@P12: Rosenblatt said he could explain why the machine learned only in\nL102@P12: highly technical terms” (italics mine). \u001a e story, however, had none\nL103@P12: of the “highly technical” details.\nL104@P12: \u001a is book does. It tackles the technical details. It explains the\nL105@P12: elegant mathematics and algorithms that have, for decades, ener\u0002gized and excited researchers in “machine learning,” a type of AI\nL106@P12: that involves building machines that can learn to discern patterns in\nL107@P12: data without being explicitly programmed to do so. Trained ma\u0002chines can then detect similar patterns in new, previously unseen\nL108@P12: data, making possible applications that range from recognizing pic\u0002tures of cats and dogs to creating, potentially, autonomous cars and\nL109@P12: other technology. Machines can learn because of the extraordinary\nL110@P12: con\u0016 uence of math and computer science, with more than a dash of\nL111@P12: physics and neuroscience added to the mix.\nL112@P12: Machine learning (ML) is a vast \u0018 eld populated by algorithms\nL113@P12: that leverage relatively simple math that goes back centuries, math\nL114@P12: one learns in high school or early in college. \u001a ere’s, of course, ele\u0002mentary algebra. Another extremely important cornerstone of ma\u0002chine learning is calculus, co-invented by no less a polymath than\nL115@P12: Isaac Newton. \u001a e \u0018 eld also relies heavily on the work of \u001a omas\nL116@P12: Bayes, the eighteenth- century English statistician and minister who\nL117@P12: gave us the eponymous Bayes’s theorem, a key contribution to the\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn53view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.gbv.de/dms/weimar/toc/1690307358_toc.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://www.gbv.de/dms/weimar/toc/1690307358_toc.pdf: (429) Too Many Requests\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://cdn.penguin.co.uk/dam-assets/books/9781802062076/9781802062076-sample.pdf)\nciteturn53view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 29; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://cdn.penguin.co.uk/dam-assets/books/9781802062076/9781802062076-sample.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1302\nL0@P0: ‘Riveting, essential for understanding how economic\nL1@P0: and technological power is wielded today’\nL2@P0: Chris Miller, author of Chip War\nL3@P0: UNDERGROUND\nL4@P0: EMPIRE\nL5@P0: How America Weaponized\nL6@P0: the World Economy\nL7@P0: HENRY FARRELL\nL8@P0-1: ABRAHAM NEWMANPENGUIN BOOKS\nL9@P1: UNDERGROUND EMPIRE\nL10@P1: ‘The publication of Underground Empire could not be more timely. Henry\nL11@P1: Farrell and Abraham Newman offer an important corrective to a dominant\nL12@P1: narrative in US foreign policy circles that positions the US and other Western\nL13@P1: governments as innocent by-standers, caught off-guard by their main rivals’\nL14@P1: Emily Jones, Times Literary Supplement\nL15@P1: ‘Revelatory . . . the underground empire deserves the same kind of\nL16@P1: sophisticated thinking once devoted to nuclear rivalries . . . by highlighting\nL17@P1: how the nature of global power has changed, the book makes an enormous\nL18@P1: contribution to the way analysts think about influence’\nL19@P1: Paul Krugman, Foreign Affairs\nL20@P1: ‘The US has made use of a novel, often mysterious set of tools for\nL21@P1: rewarding those who help it and punishing those who cross it. That set of\nL22@P1: tools is now a bit less mysterious, thanks to Henry Farrell and Abraham\nL23@P1: Newman. Their book Underground Empire reveals how the US benefits\nL24@P1: from a set of institutions built up late last century as neutral means of\nL25@P1: streamlining global markets’ Christopher Caldwell, The New York Times\nL26@P1: ‘Farrell and Newman describe the rise over the past 50 years of what\nL27@P1: they call America’s “network imperialism.” In an era where markets\nL28@P1: were supposedly becoming ever-more disembedded from states, the authors\nL29@P1: show that the opposite was the case. . . . The vision one\nL30@P1: leaves their book with is one of great-power conflict where, as usual, those at\nL31@P1: the bottom of the world’s hierarchy of wealth continue to\nL32@P1: suffer the most, with no refuge in sight’\nL33@P1: Quinn Slobodian, New Statesman\nL34@P1: ‘The sharpest and most striking analysis I’ve seen in years of the state the\nL35@P1: world’s in, cunningly disguised as a user-friendly business book’\nL36@P1: Francis Spufford, author of Golden Hill\nL37@P1: ‘Henry Farrell and Abraham Newman paint a persuasively alarming picture\nL38@P1: of just how American power has become entrenched deep in the plumbing of\nL39@P1: the world economy. Underground Empire is a passionate plea for restraint\nL40@P1: and reform in the face of a world burdened by all kinds of geopolitical\nL41@P1: dangers’ Helen Thompson\nL42@P1: ‘Farrell and Newman’s book is like an MRI or CT scan of recent world\nL43@P1: history, giving us a new and startling image of the global body politic, as\nL44@P1: clear as an X-ray. Cognitive mapping takes on a new aspect with their\nL45@P1: analysis, as they shift from the technological to the historical, showing both\nL46@P1: how this new nervous system of world power came to be, and how it could\nL47@P1: be put to better use than it is now. Given the intertwined complexities of our\nL48@P1: very dangerous polycrisis, we need their insights’\nL49@P1: Kim Stanley Robinson, author of The Ministry for the Future\nL50@P1: ‘Underground Empire tells a riveting story about the deep forces that have\nL51@P1: shaped our present moment. The book is a portrait not of a single\nL52@P1: protagonist or event, but rather a system that shapes much of the world\nL53@P1: today: a web of dollars and data that has, half accidentally, given the United\nL54@P1: States a new kind of geopolitical control over both its enemies and allies. It is\nL55@P1: history written in its most powerful form: a view of the recent past that gives\nL56@P1: us a new lens to better discern our future’\nL57@P1: Steven Johnson, author of How We Got to Now\nL58@P1-2: Copyrighted Material‘Underground Empire is an astonishing explanation of how power really\nL59@P2: works. From fiber optic cables to the financial system, Farrell and Newman\nL60@P2: show how the networks that knit us together are also powerful coercive\nL61@P2: tools, providing a subtle and revelatory account of how the United States\nL62@P2: learned to weaponize its dominance of the world order’s plumbing. A\nL63@P2: riveting read, essential for understanding how economic and technological\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://cdn.penguin.co.uk/dam-assets/books/9781529936520/9781529936520-sample.pdf)\nciteturn53view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 27; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn52view0\",\"pattern\":\"Notes\"}); Total lines: 439\nL222@P15-16: Copyrighted Materialxvi introduction\nL223@P16: write a college essay, solve a tricky math problem, provide an expert medical\nL224@P16: diagnosis, and cohost a podcast. It scales with the amount of computing power\nL225@P16: available to it and never seems to plateau. The evening before our breakfast,\nL226@P16: I’d watched a video in which a robot, running this new kind of software,\nL227@P16: stared at its hands in seeming recognition, then sorted a collection of col\u0002ored blocks. The video had given me chills; the obsolescence of my species\nL228@P16: seemed near. Huang, rolling a pancake around a sausage with his fingers,\nL229@P16: dismissed my concerns. “I know how it works, so there’s nothing there,” he\nL230@P16: said. “It’s no different than how microwaves work.” I pressed Huang— an\nL231@P16: autonomous robot surely presents risks that a microwave oven does not. He\nL232@P16: responded that he has never worried about the technology, not once. “All it’s\nL233@P16: doing is processing data,” he said. “There are so many other things to worry\nL234@P16: about.”\nL235@P16: Where this will lead is anyone’s guess; many technologists now worry\nL236@P16: that AI’s capabilities pose a direct threat to the survival of the human spe\u0002cies. (Among these “doomers” are the Toronto scientists who first imple\u0002mented AI on Huang’s platform.) Huang dismisses such pessimism. For\nL237@P16: him, AI is a pure force for progress, and he has declared that it is spur\u0002ring a new industrial revolution. He doesn’t permit much disagreement on\nL238@P16: this topic, and his force of personality can be intimidating. (“Interacting\nL239@P16: with Jensen is like sticking your finger in the electrical socket,” one of his\nL240@P16: executives said.) Huang’s employees worship him— I believe they would fol\u0002low him out of the window of a skyscraper if he saw a market opportunity\nL241@P16: there.\nL242@P16: In May 2023, hundreds of industry leaders endorsed a statement that\nL243@P16: equated the risk of runaway AI with that of nuclear war. Huang didn’t sign\nL244@P16: it. Some economists have observed that the Industrial Revolution led to a\nL245@P16: relative decline in the global population of horses and have wondered if AI\nL246@P16: might do the same to humans. “Horses have limited career options,” Huang\nL247@P16: said. “For example, horses can’t type.” As he finished eating, I expressed my\nL248@P16: concerns that, someday soon, I would feed my notes from our conversation\nL249@P16: into an intelligence engine, then watch as it produced structured, superior\nL250@P16: prose. Huang didn’t dismiss this possibility, but he assured me that I had a\nL251@P16-17: Copyrighted Material introduction xvii\nL252@P17: few years before my John Henry moment. “It will come for the fiction writ\u0002ers first,” he said. Then he tipped the waitress a thousand dollars and stood\nL253@P17: up from his many plates of half- eaten food.\nL254@P17: I found Huang to be an elusive subject, in some ways the most difficult\nL255@P17: I’ve ever reported on. He hates talking about himself and once responded to\nL256@P17: one of my questions by physically running away. Before this book was com\u0002missioned, I had written a magazine profile of Huang for The New Yorker.\nL257@P17: Huang told me he hadn’t read it, and had no intention of ever doing so. In\u0002formed that I was writing a biography of him, he responded, “I hope I die\nL258@P17: before it comes out.”\nL259@P17: Still, Huang offered me access to a great number of people to report this\nL260@P17: book. I spoke with almost two hundred people, including his employees, his\nL261@P17: cofounders, his rivals, and several of his oldest friends. The beloved and\nL262@P17: even somewhat goofy family man who emerged from these interviews bore\nL263@P17: little resemblance to the unapologetically carnivorous executive who made\nL264@P17: Nvidia succeed, but it is these same attachments that spur Huang’s ambi\u0002tion: he spoke frankly with me of his insecurities, his fear of letting his\nL265@P17:  employees down, his fear of bringing shame to the family name. Some ex\u0002ecutives speak of profit as “keeping score,” but not Huang; for him, the\nL266@P17: money is only temporary insurance against some future calamity. There\nL267@P17: was something a little touching about hearing a man worth a hundred bil\u0002lion dollars talk in this way.\nL268@P17: But if Huang is motivated by anxiety, he is also motivated by fascination\nL269@P17: with the seductive power his technology has unlocked. He had not set out to\nL270@P17: be an AI pioneer, not even when he’d turned his attention to parallel com\u0002puting, but once it arrived, Huang became determined to push his maxi\u0002malist agenda for machine intelligence as far and as fast as it could possibly\nL271@P17: go. Even the most optimistic visionaries in the field urge some degree of\nL272@P17: caution; the supposed mission of OpenAI, for example, is to ward off catas\u0002trophe. Huang, almost alone, believes that AI can lead only to good, and it\nL273@P17: is this belief that motivates him to work twelve to fourteen hours a day,\nL274@P17: seven days a week, even after three decades as CEO.\nL275@P17: Of course, Huang would work hard anyway. It is in his nature. If there is\nL276@P17-18: Copyrighted Materialxviii introduction\nL277@P18: a theme to his life, it is amplification; he has executed on the same simple\nL278@P18: precepts of diligence, courage, and mastery of fundamentals again and again\nL279@P18: and again, to greater and greater effect. I was surprised to learn how much\nL280@P18: of the man he later became was present in the immigrant child arriving\nL281@P18:  unaccompanied by his parents in the United States in 1973 to an environ\u0002ment so unconducive to flourishing that it seems a miracle he survived it. To\nL282@P18: understand Huang fully, we begin not at Denny’s restaurant, nor in the\nL283@P18: giant cathedrals of technology he later commissioned, but at this tiny rural\nL284@P18: school.\nL285@P18-19: Copyrighted MaterialPART I\nL286@P19-21: Copyrighted MaterialCopyrighted Materialone\nL287@P21: The Bridge\nL288@P21: S\nL289@P21: ometime toward the end of 1973, ten- year- old Jensen Huang rose\nL290@P21: from bed in his dormitory and set off on the perilous journey to school.\nL291@P21: Huang, born in Taiwan and raised in Thailand, had recently arrived\nL292@P21: in rural Kentucky. His path led down a sloping hillside to a floodplain situ\u0002ated among forested hills, and across a rickety pedestrian footbridge, which\nL293@P21: was suspended by ropes and missing many planks, through which could be\nL294@P21: seen the frigid and rushing waters of the river below.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://nicolefv.com/s/accelerate-book-excerpt.pdf)\nciteturn53view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today;