at another reason\nL200@P10: for not tossing it was that I was reading it on my iPad.)\nL201@P10: So, instead I emailed Jez to find out what lay behind this\nL202@P10: statement. A few weeks later I was on a call with him and Nicole\nL203@P10: Forsgren, who patiently walked me though the reasoning. While\nL204@P10: I’m no expert on the methods they used, she said enough to con\u0002vince me there was some real analysis going on here, far more\nL205@P10: than I usually see, even in academic papers. I followed the sub\u0002sequent State of DevOps reports with interest, but also with\nL206@P10: growing frustration. The reports gave the results of their work but\nL207@P10: never contained the explanation that Nicole walked through with\nL208@P10: me on the phone. This greatly undermined their credibility, as\nL209@P10: there was little evidence that these reports were based on more\nL210@P10: than speculation. Finally, those of us that had seen behind the\nL211@P10: curtains convinced Nicole, Jez, and Gene to reveal their methods\nL212@P10-11: FOREWORD | xiby writing this book. For me, it’s been a long wait, but I’m glad I\nL213@P11: now have something that I can genuinely recommend as a way to\nL214@P11: look at IT delivery effectiveness—one that’s based on more than\nL215@P11: a few analysts’ scattered experiences.\nL216@P11: The picture they paint is compelling. They describe how effective\nL217@P11: IT delivery organizations take about an hour to get code from\nL218@P11: “committed to mainline” to “running in production,” a journey\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://nicolefv.com/s/accelerate-book-excerpt.pdf)\nciteturn53view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 93; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn52view1\",\"pattern\":\"REFERENCES\"}); Total lines: 2194\nNo matching text found for \"REFERENCES\""
